<p>
Rick Manelius has proposed a new acronym: <a href="https://www.rickmanelius.com/p/aidr-ai-didnt-read">AI;DR</a>. It stands for &ldquo;AI; Didn&rsquo;t Read&rdquo;. He says that it actually originated with @seclilc <a href="https://x.com/seclilc/status/2088660446270128324?s=20)">here</a>. Manelius isn&rsquo;t an AI hater and, if fact, uses it himself. It&rsquo;s just that he objects to asking someone a question and getting back an unedited—and probably unread—answer from a chat bot in reply. His new policy, he says, is that if you can&rsquo;t be bothered to read and edit it, then he&rsquo;s not going to bother reading it.
</p>
<p>
Really, responding to someone&rsquo;s question with a buffer full of AI slop is profoundly disrespectful and will cause you to forfeit the goodwill and respect of your victim. If you want a less touchy-feely explanation, Manelius has an update pointing to <a href="https://dontpastetheai.com/angry/">dontpastetheai</a>, which expresses the same sentiment in less circumspect language.
</p>
<p>
I&rsquo;m sympathetic to all of this. If I ask someone a question, it&rsquo;s because I want the benefit of <i>their</i> wisdom not some distilled twaddle that may not even be correct. If they don&rsquo;t know or don&rsquo;t have time at the moment to provide a detailed answer, I&rsquo;m fine with their saying so. Just don&rsquo;t send me some slop that even they can&rsquo;t be bothered reading.</p>
